                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mb-4">Health &amp; Research Data Platforms</h2>
                <p class="lead">Interactive reporting for organizations whose data currently reaches its audience as static documents.</p>

                <p>Registries, annual data reports and outcomes studies produce enormous, dense datasets. The analysis is already good. What is usually missing is the layer between that analysis and the clinician, patient, board member or policymaker who has to make a decision with it.</p>

                <p>That layer is what I build. I have done it for the Scientific Registry of Transplant Recipients and the United States Renal Data System, on tools that patients and providers use to make real clinical decisions. R and SAS output goes in, a fast interactive application comes out.</p>

                <h3 class="h4 mt-4 mb-3">What This Usually Looks Like:</h3>
                <ul class="mb-4">
                    <li><strong>Interactive Report Builds:</strong> Replacing static PDF reports with filterable, linkable, fast web applications</li>
                    <li><strong>Analysis Pipeline Integration:</strong> Taking R, SAS or Python output and turning it into a queryable data layer</li>
                    <li><strong>Data Visualization:</strong> Charting and mapping that holds up under scrutiny from the people who produced the numbers</li>
                    <li><strong>Public and Patient-Facing Tools:</strong> Search, comparison and calculator interfaces for non-technical audiences</li>
                    <li><strong>Annual Cycle Support:</strong> Rebuilding and republishing when the next data cycle lands</li>
                </ul>

                <p>More on <a href="/services/health-data-platforms">health and research data platforms</a>, or see the <a href="/portfolio">portfolio</a> for live examples.</p>
