    <section id="umbraco-migrations" class="mb-5 pb-4 border-bottom">
        <div class="row">
            <div class="col-md-8 offset-md-2">
                <h2 class="h3 fw-bold mb-4">Umbraco Version Migrations</h2>
                <p class="lead">If you are stranded on Umbraco 7 or 8 with a .NET Framework backend, this is a fixed-scope engagement with a known ending.</p>

                <p>The jump from Umbraco 7 or 8 to a current version is not an upgrade. It is a rewrite of the backend, a migration of the content tree, a replacement of every legacy property editor and a move from .NET Framework to .NET Core. There is no in-place path, which is why many organizations are still on an unsupported version.</p>

                <p>I led this migration for the Minnesota Secretary of State, moving the state's official website from Umbraco 7 to 14 and .NET Framework to .NET Core.</p>

                <h3 class="h4 mt-4 mb-3">What a Migration Engagement Covers:</h3>
                <ul class="mb-4">
                    <li><strong>Assessment:</strong> An honest inventory of what migrates cleanly, what gets rebuilt and what gets dropped</li>
                    <li><strong>Content Migration:</strong> Document types, templates and the full content tree, with history preserved where possible</li>
                    <li><strong>Backend Rewrite:</strong> .NET Framework to .NET Core, including custom controllers, surface controllers and services</li>
                    <li><strong>Property Editor Replacement:</strong> Legacy grid, macro and custom editors rebuilt on the current API</li>
                    <li><strong>Cutover Planning:</strong> Sequencing, rollback plan and a launch that does not take the site down</li>
                </ul>

                <p>I also work white-label for agencies with legacy Umbraco clients. More on <a href="/services/umbraco-consulting">Umbraco migrations and consulting</a>.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

